' The body of gateway-internals.puml / gateway-internals-dark.puml. Not a diagram on its own: it
' carries no start marker, so PlantUML will not render this file directly.
'
' Careful: the start and end markers must not appear in this file at all, not
' even inside a comment. PlantUML scans for them lexically, so a comment naming
' one opens a second diagram here and the include fails with a line number
' pointing at neither file. See README.md.
'
' Split out because the README ships a light and a dark render of this diagram
' and the two must not drift. Every colour is a variable the wrapper supplies,
' so the dark render is dark throughout rather than a dark canvas behind white
' cards, which is worse than shipping no dark render at all.
'
' The post-render passes in README.md are not optional for either half.

' A post-render step widens this single font name into a fallback stack, so a
' viewer without Inter gets a sans-serif rather than the monospace a missing
' font can fall back to.
skinparam defaultFontName Inter

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 20
skinparam ranksep 48
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title The gateway: five parts, and what it deliberately does not know

header
The gateway is the <b>only</b> component holding a client's TCP socket, and it never parses a payload.
Those two facts give ordering and config-driven routing for free.
endheader

actor "client" as client $c_blue
component "**services**\ngRPC" as svc $c_green

package "starling-gateway" as GW $c_gw {
  component "**event loop**  «reactor»\nthe one place that awaits socket readiness" as loop $c_card
  component "**listener**\nTCP accept · TLS handshake\nmints the connection id\n--\n**requests a client certificate**, accepts any\nissuer, hands the SHA-1 to the services" as listener $c_card
  component "**connection**  [0..*]\none TLS stream · control framing\n«type u16 + len u32»\n--\n**two bounded queues, two policies:**\ncontrol full -> **disconnect**\naudio full -> **drop oldest, count**" as conn $c_card
  component "**router**\nreads the u16 type, looks it up in TOML,\nforwards the payload **verbatim**.\n**No generated stubs. No schemas.**" as router $c_hub
  component "**limiter**\na bucket **per route**, never one shared" as lim $c_hub
  component "**breaker + shedder**\na circuit breaker per service, and\ninbound shed by `tier` when one is unhealthy" as brk $c_hub
  component "**resume buffer**\nsequence number per session,\nreplayed on RESUME" as res $c_gw2
}

client -down-> listener : TCP 64738
listener *-- conn : on accept
loop --> listener : accept readiness
loop --> conn : read · write readiness
conn --> lim : inbound frame
lim --> brk : if it passed the bucket
brk --> router : if the tier is healthy
router -[$l_blue]-> svc : gRPC, payload unchanged
svc -[$l_blue]-> res : outbound frames
res --> conn : stamped with a sequence number

note right of brk $c_hub
  **Deadlines alone fail slowly.** A saturated service makes
  every caller wait the full deadline and *then* fail, burning
  gateway capacity the whole time. Trip the breaker and shed
  at the door instead.
  Shedding uses the same `tier` the readiness logic does:
  essential unhealthy rejects logins, optional unhealthy is
  invisible.
end note

note right of res $c_gw2
  **Why a replay buffer and not just a reconnect.** Draining a
  pod with 10 000 clients means 10 000 simultaneous full floods
  of every ChannelState and UserState, a self-inflicted DDoS on
  the services behind. A client that resumes from its last
  sequence number replays only the gap.
  The buffer is therefore *meant* to live **outside** this
  process, so a resuming client can land on any pod.
  **«not built»: today it is a `HashMap` in this process.** It
  survives a reconnect to the same pod and nothing more, so with
  more than one gateway RESUME does not help the case it exists
  for. `zstd` on the Fancy stream is likewise a dependency no
  source file uses yet.
  Legacy clients cannot resume either way and take the full
  flood, which is why staggered drain and jittered reconnect
  hints are required regardless of this buffer.
end note

note right of router $c_hub
  **Why the gateway can stay ignorant.**
  The type is in the *framing*, not the protobuf
  (Server.cpp:2040 writes it as a big-endian u16).
  So routing needs two bytes and a lookup table.
  A new service is a TOML block; this binary
  never changes and links no service's code.
end note

note right of lim $c_hub
  **Per route, and never silent.**
  murmur has ONE bucket per user - 1 msg/s,
  burst 5, silent drop. A screen-share start
  emits several signalling messages back to
  back, and this ate the loopback SDP offer in
  most runs: client logged success, server
  logged nothing.
  Fancy clients are told they were throttled.
  Legacy clients keep the silence they expect.
end note

note bottom of conn
  **Single writer per client socket.** Every frame to a client
  leaves through the one process that holds its connection, so
  per-client ordering is preserved by construction - `ChannelState`
  cannot arrive after the `UserState` naming it, whichever service
  produced them. No lane rule and no version counter needed.
end note

note right of listener $c_blue
  **Why it asks for a certificate it will not validate.**
  In Mumble a certificate *is* the identity: a registered account
  is bound to one, a ban can be issued against one, and a client
  presenting the right one is admitted without a password.
  None of that is reachable unless the server sends a
  `CertificateRequest`: with `with_no_client_auth()` it never
  does, so every peer arrives anonymous and all three mechanisms
  are silently unreachable.
  Clients are self-signed by design, so **any issuer is accepted**
  and the hash is treated as an *identifier*, not as a third
  party's opinion. Possession of the private key is still proved
  in the handshake, so the hash cannot simply be copied.
  `strong_cert` is set only when a chain verified against a
  configured CA, and it is the only field an operator should key
  a privilege off.
end note

note bottom of loop
  **Why the loop is separate from the wire format.**
  "Await readiness and pump gRPC" and "speak Mumble's framing"
  are two jobs. Fused, they become one file nobody can review.
end note

note bottom of listener $c_hub
  **Every way a connection ends must reach one place.**
  The read loop breaks on a clean close, on an I/O error, a
  crashed client, a reset, a dropped network, which is how *most*
  disconnects actually arrive, and on a service asking for the
  client to go. All three run the same teardown: forget the
  registry entry, tell the services, count it.
  Skipping it on any path leaves the connection in the registry
  and every other client rendering a user who has gone.
end note

note left of conn $c_green
  **Why the two queue policies differ.** Dropping a control
  message desyncs that client permanently and silently - it
  renders the wrong world forever with nothing in any log.
  Queueing without a bound is a memory DoS. Disconnecting is
  the only bounded outcome, and reconnect re-syncs from scratch.
  A late **audio** frame is worthless, so drop it and count it.
end note

note as N1 $c_neutral
  **Not here, on purpose:**
  · the **cipher** - it lives in voice, with the UDP socket,
    so one hop can seal for every listener
  · **audio routing** - clients send UDP straight to voice
  · **any service's schema** - see the router note
end note

legend bottom
| solid, one arrowhead | a direct call inside the gateway |
| <color:$l_blue>blue</color> | gRPC to or from a service |
| filled diamond | creates and owns |
endlegend
